WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.061
What is generative AI?

00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:08.453
An AI agent has two parts:
a brain that thinks, and tools that act.

00:00:08.757 --> 00:00:10.731
Today, we look inside the brain.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:16.267
In one sentence, generative AI is
a very powerful word-chain game.

00:00:16.481 --> 00:00:20.522
Let's see how it works,
and why it can sometimes make mistakes.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.288
Computers can't read words, only numbers.

00:00:24.328 --> 00:00:27.644
So every word is turned into a number,
using a dictionary.

00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:30.051
These pieces are called tokens.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.723
And the dictionaries are big.

00:00:32.979 --> 00:00:36.108
GPT-3 has about 50,000 tokens.

00:00:36.298 --> 00:00:38.817
GPT-4, about 100,000.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.855
And GPT-5, about 150,000.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.911
Here, "Taiwan's best"
becomes three numbers.

00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:50.064
The language model predicts
the next number, 14221,

00:00:50.131 --> 00:00:53.121
and we decode it back into a word: "food."

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.237
That's how it answers.

00:00:55.663 --> 00:00:58.480
We ask, "What is Taiwan's
tallest mountain?"

00:00:58.729 --> 00:01:00.311
The model predicts "Yu."

00:01:00.835 --> 00:01:03.776
We add it to the question
and ask again: "shan."

00:01:04.214 --> 00:01:08.115
Add that too,
and it predicts END, so it stops.

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:12.615
But at each step,
there isn't just one choice.

00:01:12.897 --> 00:01:15.777
The model scores thousands
of possible next words,

00:01:15.822 --> 00:01:17.967
then rolls the dice among the likely ones.

00:01:18.358 --> 00:01:22.046
So the answer could start
with "Yu," "It," or "The."

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.867
Another example.

00:01:23.907 --> 00:01:30.188
After "Ice," the word "cream" scores 0.5,
and "tea" scores 0.2.

00:01:30.647 --> 00:01:32.848
Thousands of others score almost zero.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.553
Roll the dice,
and this time we get "cream."

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.200
This is why the answer
is different every time.

00:01:40.708 --> 00:01:42.538
It's also why mistakes can appear.

00:01:42.677 --> 00:01:44.392
We call them hallucinations.

00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:49.242
One unlucky word can be chosen,
and every later word follows it.

00:01:49.543 --> 00:01:53.888
Pick "Ali" instead of "Yu," and the
model confidently writes "Alishan":

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:55.076
the wrong mountain.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:02:00.769
The lesson is simple: AI sounds confident,
but we must check its work.

00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:04.063
So how do we know which AI is good?

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.899
This chart ranks many models
with one independent index,

00:02:07.974 --> 00:02:10.158
dated November 12, 2026.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.750
The top scores come from
a few different companies,

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.268
and they are close to each other.

00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:19.618
Open-weight models, whose weights
anyone can download, sit a bit lower.

00:02:19.992 --> 00:02:22.507
There is no single winner for every job.

00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:29.606
So remember: generative AI plays word
chain, rolls the dice, and can be wrong.

00:02:30.237 --> 00:02:33.108
Use more than one model,
and let them check each other.

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:36.650
Next, we give the brain a suit of tools.
